   links use "help#…" (not "#…"): a bare fragment would resolve against the base address and go to the Run tab. *@

<PageTitle>Help · GS Test Harness</PageTitle>

<div class="help-page">
    <header class="help-header">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h1>Help</h1>
        <span class="help-version">GS Test Harness v@(typeof(Program).Assembly.GetName().Version?.ToString(3))</span>
    </header>

    <div class="help-body">
        <nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Contents">
            <ol>
                <li><a href="help#start">Getting started</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#servers">The server list</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#run">The Run tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#sites">Sites</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#conformu">ConformU Debug Log Test</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#tiers">Diagnostic tiers</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#options">Options</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#results">Reading the results</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#reports">The Reports tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#compare">The Compare tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#recommendations">The Recommendations tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#checks">What gets checked</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#tests">The tests</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#cli">From the command line</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#files">Where things are stored</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#trouble">Troubleshooting</a></li>
            </ol>
        </nav>

        <article class="help-text">
            <p class="lead">
                GS Test Harness checks a telescope server for ASCOM compliance: <strong>GSServer</strong> through its COM
                driver, or <strong>GreenSwampAlpaca</strong> over Alpaca. It calls every ITelescopeV4 member (and, for
                Alpaca, the HTTP protocol), and for each problem says what's wrong, what ASCOM expects, how to fix it and
                where the code to change probably is.
            </p>

            <section id="start">
                <h2>Getting started</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li>Add the server to the <strong>Servers</strong> list on the left (see <a href="help#servers">The server list</a>).</li>
                    <li>Drag it onto the <strong>Server and sites</strong> card on the <strong>Run</strong> tab (or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>).</li>
                    <li>Tick the sites to run at, choose what the run may do, and press <strong>Start run</strong> beside the server.
                        A warning asks you to remove the OTAs and watch the mount for the whole run, and lists the settings under
                        the server to check first (Mount, flip angle, timeouts and source code), with ⚠ on any that look wrong. Clicking
                        <strong>Start</strong> there accepts responsibility for the run.</li>
                    <li>Read the findings as they come in. Each run is saved; open it later on the <strong>Reports</strong> tab.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="warning">Moving the mount is off by default. Turn it on only for a simulator, or a real mount that's clear to move.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="servers">
                <h2>The server list</h2>
                <p>The buttons above the list:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Add (+)</dt>
                    <dd>A new entry: a name, which server it is, and its Alpaca host, port and device number (with a user
                        name and password for a server that asks for one), or the COM driver's ProgID.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery (magnifier)</dt>
                    <dd>Searches the local network for Alpaca telescopes and lists the ones that answer. Pick one to add it.</dd>
                    <dt>ASCOM Drivers (telescope)</dt>
                    <dd>Lists the ASCOM telescope drivers installed on this computer. Windows only.</dd>
                    <dt>Connect (plug)</dt>
                    <dd>Connects to, or disconnects from, the selected server, to see that it answers. A run connects by itself.</dd>
                    <dt>Save (disk)</dt>
                    <dd>Saves the list now. It's also saved after every change.</dd>
                    <dt>Delete (bin)</dt>
                    <dd>Removes the selected server from the list. Its reports are kept.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>The dot before a server is green while it's connected. Double-click a server (or press <kbd>F2</kbd>)
                    to edit it, and press <kbd>Delete</kbd> to remove it. The <strong>«</strong> button folds the list
                    into a narrow strip; this browser remembers the choice.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="run">
                <h2>The Run tab</h2>
                <p>Three tabs run across the top: <strong>ASCOM Conformance Check</strong> (a full check),
                    <strong>ConformU Debug Log Test</strong> (following up a ConformU debug log, see <a href="help#conformu">ConformU Debug Log Test</a>)
                    and <strong>Options</strong> (what a run may do, for both). Under <strong>ASCOM Conformance Check</strong> a second
                    row holds its pages: <strong>Run</strong>, <strong>Reports</strong>, <strong>Compare</strong>
                    and <strong>Recommendations</strong>. Under <strong>ConformU Debug Log Test</strong>
                    the same row holds the pages its runs use: <strong>Run</strong>, <strong>Reports</strong>
                    and <strong>Recommendations</strong> (not Compare). Each tab's pages
                    show only its own runs: under ConformU Debug Log Test, Reports and Recommendations show only ConformU runs
                    (the ones that followed up a log); under ASCOM Conformance Check, Reports, Compare and Recommendations
                    show only full conformance runs. The check groups a run makes are listed under
                    <a href="help#checks">What gets checked</a>, and every check in them under <a href="help#tests">The tests</a>.</p>
                <p>The <strong>Server and sites</strong> card holds the server to test. Drag one onto it from the list;
                    <strong>✕</strong> clears it. Under the server, on both tests:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Source code</dt>
                    <dd>Where the server's source code is, so findings can name a file (see
                        <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>).</dd>
                    <dt>Mount</dt>
                    <dd>What the server drives: <strong>GSServer simulator</strong> or a <strong>Real mount</strong>. GSServer takes
                        different code paths for each, so the ConformU diagnoses' recommendations follow it; not specified, they
                        list both. It's remembered as soon as it's changed. On the command line, <code>--mount simulator</code>
                        or <code>--mount real</code>.</dd>
                    <dt>Flip angle</dt>
                    <dd>Degrees either side of the meridian (0 to 90) where the mount can point from either side of the pier.
                        Inside it the flip checks flip the mount with the SideOfPier command, halfway in on each side, and it must
                        keep pointing at the same place. A goto 5° past it, across the meridian, must flip the mount by itself.
                        Needs <em>Move the mount</em> (under Options). Remembered as soon as it is changed.</dd>
                    <dt>Slew timeout and call timeout</dt>
                    <dd>How long a slew, and any single call, may take before it counts as never answered. Remembered as soon as they are changed.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p><strong>Start run</strong> sits beside the server. With more than one site ticked it reads
                    <strong>Start 2 runs</strong> or <strong>Start 3 runs</strong>: one run, and one report, per site.
                    While it runs, a bar shows the step and progress, with <strong>Stop</strong> to end it early (the mount
                    is still put back). The findings appear below as they come in.</p>
                <p>When it's done, the bar links to each report and, for several sites, to <strong>Compare sites</strong>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="conformu">
                <h2>ConformU Debug Log Test</h2>
                <p>Drop a ConformU debug log on the <strong>ConformU debug log</strong> card, or click it to choose one.
                    It must be a debug log: run ConformU with debug logging turned on in its settings. A normal log (one
                    with no DEBUG lines) is turned away with a note saying so. Every ERROR, ISSUE, CONFIGURATION ALERT and WARNING line is read (the repeats in ConformU's
                    end-of-run summary count once) and grouped by the ITelescopeV4 member it is about. The table shows, for
                    each, what ConformU said and which harness checks look at it.</p>
                <p><strong>Run ConformU checks</strong>, beside the server, checks only the members ConformU found problems
                    with. It runs the groups that check them, and within each group only the parts that touch those members
                    (for a PulseGuide problem, Motion's pulse-guide moves but not its slews, syncs or flips), plus the quick
                    read-only groups they depend on (Connection, Common, Capabilities, Properties, Methods, Performance).
                    Only findings about the problem members are reported, with anything that explains a check not made. Extended checks
                    are turned on when a problem needs them; moving the mount and writing settings stay as set under
                    <strong>Options</strong>, and the card says which problems they would leave unchecked. Sites aren't
                    used: it's one run at the server's own site, so the tab has no site ticks.</p>
                <p>The <strong>ConformU</strong> findings then give a verdict for each problem:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Reproduced (Fail or Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>A harness check of the same member found a problem: ConformU's problem is real, and the harness's fix applies.
                        For a member the harness diagnoses (see Diagnoses below), only the diagnosis counts: it found the cause in the driver.</dd>
                    <dt>Explained (Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>Diagnosed members only: the member works, and ConformU's result comes from something else, such as a stale or
                        coarse position reading. The diagnosis's recommendation applies.</dd>
                    <dt>Not reproduced (Info)</dt>
                    <dd>Every harness check of the member passed, or its diagnosis found it within tolerance: fixed since the ConformU run,
                        or ConformU tested something the harness doesn't.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>For a diagnosed member, other problems the harness's checks find with it (a wrong error type, say) are listed in the
                    verdict as <em>also found</em>: they're real, but they aren't the problem ConformU reported.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Not checked (Skipped)</dt>
                    <dd>The checks that would look at it didn't run (motion off, for example). Turn it on and run again.</dd>
                    <dt>Generated check (Probe)</dt>
                    <dd>A property no harness check reached in this run is read three times, and its values, errors and read times reported.</dd>
                    <dt>No check (Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>Nothing here can test it safely (a ConformU-only topic): reproduce it by hand.</dd>
                </dl>
                <h3>Diagnoses</h3>
                <p>This run is tier 1: it finds the problems. Rate offset and pulse guide problems can then be taken further
                    at tiers 2 and 3 (see <a href="help#tiers">Diagnostic tiers</a>), whose findings
                    (<strong>ConformU.Diagnose…</strong>) decide the verdict for those members.</p>
                <p>Set <strong>Mount</strong> under the server (on the Server card) so the recommendations point at the right
                    code: GSServer's simulator (GS.Simulator) or a real mount (GS.SkyWatcher). Not specified, they list both.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="tiers">
                <h2>Diagnostic tiers</h2>
                <p>Both tests go in tiers, each deeper than the one before. Tier 1 always runs first (<strong>Start run</strong>
                    and <strong>Run ConformU checks</strong> are tier 1); tiers 2 and 3 are offered under the results, only for
                    problems the tier below found that have a deeper test.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>ASCOM Conformance Check</th><th>ConformU Debug Log Test</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @for (var tier = 1; tier <= Tiers.Max; tier++)
                        {
                            <tr><td>@Tiers.Name(tier)</td><td>@Tiers.Purpose(tier, false)</td><td>@Tiers.Purpose(tier, true)</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Tier 3 ends with a <strong>Summary</strong> finding for each member: whether it confirms or overturns tier 2's
                    diagnosis and recommendation, the final analysis, and the recommended fix.</p>
                <p>A tier 2 or 3 run can't be started any other way: it follows up the run just below it (same device, same site,
                    the options as set now), and its report says which run that was. Its findings are the
                    <strong>Diagnose…</strong> group for a conformance run, <strong>ConformU.Diagnose…</strong> for a ConformU
                    run. Compare uses tier 1 runs only; Recommendations add each tier 1 run's deeper findings, the deepest tier's
                    where a check was made at more than one.</p>
                <p>A conformance run's tier 2 also takes apart its other kinds of problem: invalid arguments, members that work
                    when they must refuse (tracking off, during a slew), members that answer while disconnected, SlewSettleTime, AxisRates against CanMoveAxis, DriverVersion and the COM registration. Each
                    tests around the problem (the other values, the sibling members, the states around it) and, when the
                    server's source is set under <strong>Options</strong>, reviews its code: the guards a member calls, lines
                    commented out, where a setting is used. The finding names the core issue and the change to make. The
                    measurements (rate offsets, pulse guiding), the preconditions and the settle time move the mount, so they
                    need <strong>Move the mount</strong>; the others run without it.</p>
                <p>Each diagnosis and the checks it follows up:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Member</th><th>Tests</th><th>Follows up</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var d in Tiers.Diagnoses)
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td>@d.Tier</td>
                                <td>@d.Member</td>
                                <td class="mono small">@foreach (var key in d.Produces) { <div><a href="help#test-@TestNumbers.For(key)">@TestNumbers.For(key)</a></div> }</td>
                                <td class="mono small">@foreach (var key in d.FollowsUp) { <div><a href="help#test-@TestNumbers.For(key)">@TestNumbers.For(key)</a> @key.Replace("{member}", d.Member)</div> }</td>
                                <td>@d.What</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h3 id="tier-numbers">Test numbers by tier</h3>
                <p>Every test has its own number (the <strong>No.</strong> column under <a href="help#tests">The tests</a>).
                    Its first digit is its tier: tier 1 tests are numbered from 1001, tier 2 from 2001, tier 3 from 3001.
                    A number stays with its test, and a new test takes the next number of its tier.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Tests</th><th>Numbers</th><th>Referenced by the other tiers</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @for (var tier = 1; tier <= Tiers.Max; tier++)
                        {
                            var numbers = TestCatalog.Entries.Where(e => e.Tier == tier).Select(e => e.Number).Order().ToList();
                            var referenced = TestCatalog.FollowedUp(tier);
                            <tr>
                                <td>@Tiers.Name(tier)</td>
                                <td>@numbers.Count</td>
                                <td class="tier-numbers">@Ranges(numbers)</td>
                                <td>
                                    @foreach (var e in referenced)
                                    {
                                        <div class="mono small"><a href="help#test-@e.Number">@e.Number</a> @e.Group.@e.DisplayId
                                            <span class="muted">→ @string.Join(", ", TestCatalog.FollowedBy(e).Select(d => d.Number))</span></div>
                                    }
                                    @if (referenced.Count == 0) { <span class="muted small">none (the deepest tier)</span> }
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                @foreach (var conformU in new[] { false, true })
                {
                    var chains = TestCatalog.Chains().Where(c => c.ConformU == conformU).ToList();
                    <p>@(conformU ? "ConformU Debug Log Test" : "ASCOM Conformance Check"): each tier 1 test with deeper tests,
                        and the numbers that follow it up at each tier.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Tier 1</th><th>Tier 2</th><th>Tier 3</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var c in chains)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@TierTest(c.Test)</td>
                                    <td>@foreach (var e in c.Tier2) { @TierTest(e) }</td>
                                    <td>
                                        @foreach (var e in c.Tier3) { @TierTest(e) }
                                        @if (c.Tier3.Count == 0) { <span class="muted small">none yet</span> }
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                }
                <p class="muted">Every other tier 1 test has no deeper tier yet.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="sites">
                <h2>Sites</h2>
                <p>A conformance test can be made at up to three sites, ticked under the server (the ConformU Debug Log Test
                    doesn't use them):</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Server's current settings</dt>
                    <dd>Whatever the server is set to now. Nothing is changed.</dd>
                    <dt>Northern Hemisphere and Southern Hemisphere</dt>
                    <dd>A name, latitude, longitude (east positive) and elevation in metres. A run there writes
                        SiteElevation, SiteLatitude and SiteLongitude after connecting, and puts the server's own values
                        back before disconnecting, also if the run fails or is stopped.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>A northern site needs a latitude above 0, a southern one below 0. A site with a mistake can't be ticked.
                    The sites and ticks are shared by every browser tab.</p>
                <p class="warning">The Northern and Southern Hemisphere sites are turned off for now: only the server's
                    current settings are offered.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="options">
                <h2>Options</h2>
                <p>On the Run tab's <strong>Options</strong> sub-tab. They apply to both the conformance test and the ConformU Debug Log Test.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Move the mount</dt>
                    <dd>Slews, syncs, MoveAxis, PulseGuide, park and find home. Tracking and park state are put back afterwards.</dd>
                    <dt>Write settings back unchanged and try invalid values</dt>
                    <dd>Site, guide rates, tracking rate and settle time. Anything wrongly accepted is put back.</dd>
                    <dt>Call SetPark</dt>
                    <dd>Moves the saved park position to wherever the mount is pointing. Needs <em>Move the mount</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca protocol checks</dt>
                    <dd>GreenSwampAlpaca only: management API, transaction IDs, error replies, HTTP 400 and discovery.</dd>
                    <dt>Extended checks</dt>
                    <dd>Adds a few minutes: tracking drift (on, off, rate offsets), several clients at once, parallel reads and connect cycles.</dd>
                    <dt>Disconnect at the end</dt>
                    <dd>Checks NotConnected errors. Only possible when no other client holds the device.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="results">
                <h2>Reading the results</h2>
                <p>Each finding has an outcome:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge fail">Fail</span></td><td>Breaks the ASCOM rules. Fix it.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge error">Error</span></td><td>The call threw something unexpected, or never answered.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge warning">Warning</span></td><td>Allowed, but likely to trouble some clients.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge pass">Pass</span></td><td>As ASCOM expects.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge info">Info</span></td><td>Worth knowing; not a problem.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge">Skipped</span></td><td>Not checked: not supported, or not allowed by the run's options.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>The counts above the list are filters: click one to show that outcome, click more to add theirs, and click one again to take it away; with none picked, every outcome shows.
                    Narrow it further by group or with the search box. Click a finding to open it: the check's ID, what was
                    expected, what came back, <strong>how to fix</strong> it, <strong>where</strong> in the code, and a
                    reference to the ASCOM rule.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="reports">
                <h2>The Reports tab</h2>
                <p>Every finished run, newest first: when, the device and its version, the site, COM or Alpaca, and a bar
                    of fail, warning and pass. Click the device to open the report. The buttons at the end of a row
                    download it as Markdown (ready to paste into an issue) or JSON.</p>
                <p>To delete reports, tick them (or tick <strong>Select all</strong>) and press <strong>Delete checked</strong>.
                    You're asked first; deleted reports can't be brought back.</p>
                <p>A report opens with its totals and duration, the device's identity, the run's options, and the findings.
                    Its buttons download it as Markdown, JSON or <strong>CSV</strong> (opens in Excel), or open
                    <strong>Print / PDF</strong>: the whole report laid out for paper, every problem with its details, ready for
                    the browser's Print or Save as PDF.</p>
                <p>A run whose device stopped answering part way (its COM server crashed, the Alpaca server went away) stops
                    there with one <strong>CommunicationLost</strong> error that holds the last errors seen. The groups that need
                    the device are skipped, and the findings up to that point are kept. Such runs are tagged
                    <em>connection lost</em> in the list.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="compare">
                <h2>The Compare tab</h2>
                <p><strong>Sites</strong> shows how the server on the Run tab does at each ticked site, side by side: its latest run at each,
                    one column per site. Change the server or the ticks on the Run tab and it follows.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Differs</dt>
                    <dd>Another outcome, or another message, at some site: usually something that depends on the latitude
                        or longitude, such as pier side, alt/az or sidereal time.</dd>
                    <dt>Fails at every site</dt>
                    <dd>The same problem everywhere.</dd>
                    <dt>Not checked everywhere</dt>
                    <dd>The same result wherever it was made, but some site's run didn't make the check.</dd>
                    <dt>Same, no problem</dt>
                    <dd>Passed everywhere. Hidden until you click its count.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>Runs at two or more of the ticked sites are needed.</p>
                <p><strong>Versions</strong> compares two runs of the server, check by check, to see what a new driver version
                    fixed or broke. It starts with the newest run and the newest earlier run at the same site with another
                    DriverVersion; pick any two runs under Before and After. Regressed and New problem are what to look at;
                    Problem changed, Still failing, Fixed, Not checked after and New check follow. If the two runs weren't
                    made alike (another site, motion or settings writes on in one only), a note says so: those differences
                    show as checks gone or added, not as regressions. <strong>CSV</strong> downloads the comparison.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="recommendations">
                <h2>The Recommendations tab</h2>
                <p>Every check that found a problem, across the saved runs, with the recommended fix and where the code
                    probably is. Only the latest run of each device at each site counts, so a problem fixed since an
                    older run doesn't come back. Each server is listed apart, because the fix is in its own code.</p>
                <p>A check is listed once per server, with the worst result first. Under it: the fix (more than one when
                    runs gave different advice), the source locations, the ASCOM reference and each run that found it,
                    with the expected and actual values. A check with no recorded fix shows those values instead.</p>
                <p>Click a count to show failures, errors or warnings (click several to show more than one, and again to take one away); <strong>No problem</strong> also lists the
                    checks that passed. <strong>Download Markdown</strong> saves the list, ready to paste into an issue.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="checks">
                <h2>What gets checked</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var g in TestRunner.Groups)
                        {
                            <tr><td><strong>@g.Name</strong></td><td>@g.Description</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <section id="tests">
                <h2>The tests</h2>
                <p>Every check a run can make, by group: its objective, how it is tested, how the result is verified, and
                    the reference material it is based on. In IDs, words in braces stand for each member, axis, direction
                    or rate checked. Search, or pick a group, to narrow the list.</p>
                <TestCatalogView />
            </section>

            <section id="source">
                <h2>Where to check the code</h2>
                <p>A finding names the file and line to look at, such as <code>GS.Ascom.SkyTelescope/Telescope.cs:1265</code>,
                    when the server's source code is on this computer. Under the server, on either test's Server card, paste
                    the repository's top folder into <strong>source code</strong>, or click the folder button beside it to
                    choose it: the popup lists the folders on this computer (start from <code>source\repos</code>, Documents, your home
                    folder or a drive) and marks the ones that hold the server's driver file. The folder is checked for that
                    file, and the card says which folder findings will use. The path is your own: it's saved in
                    <code>source-folders.json</code> beside the reports folder, so everyone who runs the harness sets their own,
                    and it's shared by both tests.</p>
                <p>Left empty, <code>appsettings.json</code> → <code>Codebases</code> gives the default: a folder name looked for
                    beside this program's folder and its parents, so <code>GSServer</code> and <code>GreenSwampAlpaca</code>
                    cloned next to it are found by themselves. On the command line, <code>--source &lt;folder&gt;</code> sets
                    it for that run.</p>
                <p>The source adds file names to findings, and lets tier 2 review the code (quoting the lines it found). The
                    checks and the diagnoses work without it; a tier 2 finding then says the code wasn't reviewed.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="cli">
                <h2>From the command line</h2>
                <p>For scripts and build pipelines, <code>--run</code> checks one device, saves the report like a run from
                    this page, and exits:</p>
                <pre><code>GS.TestHarness --run GreenSwampAlpaca --host localhost --port 31416 --device 0 [--motion] [--md report.md]
GS.TestHarness --run GSServer [--progid ASCOM.GS.Sky.Telescope]</code></pre>
                <p>Other options: <code>--setpark</code>, <code>--no-writes</code>, <code>--no-protocol</code>,
                    <code>--no-disconnect</code>, <code>--extended</code>, <code>--drift-seconds &lt;s&gt;</code>,
                    <code>--sites server,north,south</code> (the North and South saved here), <code>--slew-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>,
                    <code>--call-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>, <code>--flip-angle &lt;degrees&gt;</code>, <code>--mount simulator|real</code>
                    and <code>--source &lt;folder&gt;</code>.</p>
                <p><code>--conformu &lt;debug log&gt;</code> runs the ConformU Debug Log Test instead: only the log's problem members,
                    with the verdicts and diagnoses, at the server's own site. For example:</p>
                <pre><code>GS.TestHarness --run GSServer --motion --mount simulator --conformu ConformU.debug.txt</code></pre>
                <p>The exit code is 0 when nothing failed, 1 when there were failures or errors, and 2 for bad arguments.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="files">
                <h2>Where things are stored</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Reports</dt>
                    <dd><code>@Store.Folder</code>, one JSON file per run (setting <code>ReportsFolder</code>).</dd>
                    <dt>Servers and sites</dt>
                    <dd><code>servers.json</code> and <code>sites.json</code> beside the reports folder. Alpaca passwords
                        are saved encrypted (with DPAPI on Windows) and never written into reports.</dd>
                    <dt>Source folders and options</dt>
                    <dd><code>source-folders.json</code> (each server's source code, see <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>)
                        and <code>last-run.json</code> (the server and options last used) beside the reports folder.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="trouble">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery finds nothing</dt>
                    <dd>The server must be on the same network, with discovery on and UDP port 32227 not blocked by a
                        firewall. Otherwise add it with + and its host and port.</dd>
                    <dt>The ASCOM Drivers button is missing</dt>
                    <dd>It's only there on Windows. A COM driver also needs the ASCOM Platform installed.</dd>
                    <dt>The Disconnected checks are skipped</dt>
                    <dd>Another client (a planetarium, a guider, GS Point3D Web) is holding the device. Close it, or
                        untick <em>Disconnect at the end</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Slews time out</dt>
                    <dd>Raise the slew timeout, or check that the mount (or simulator) is unparked and can reach the targets.</dd>
                    <dt>Findings don't name a file</dt>
                    <dd>The server's source wasn't found: see <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>.</dd>
                    <dt>Start run is greyed out</dt>
                    <dd>Tick at least one site under the server.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>A test in the tier tables: its number (linking to it under The tests) and its ID.</summary>
    private static RenderFragment TierTest(CatalogEntry e) => @<div class="mono small"><a href="help#test-@e.Number">@e.Number</a> @e.Group.@e.DisplayId</div>;

    /// <summary>"1001–1201" for a run of numbers; runs separated by commas.</summary>
    private static string Ranges(IReadOnlyList<int> numbers)
    {
        var parts = new List<string>();
        for (var i = 0; i < numbers.Count;)
        {
            var j = i;
            while (j + 1 < numbers.Count && numbers[j + 1] == numbers[j] + 1) j++;
            parts.Add(j == i ? $"{numbers[i]}" : $"{numbers[i]}–{numbers[j]}");
            i = j + 1;
        }
        return string.Join(", ", parts);
    }
}
